The naming engines come from the nazuchem git dependency, pinned at
f418fea6747aad48d9f5cb020b00b8afc55cbb31. Its repository retains the per-file
provenance ledger and complete crate-local notices and license texts:
name-to-structure is a Rust port of OPSIN by Daniel Lowe (MIT), tracking main
after 2.9.0; structure-to-name began as a port of openclatura 0.4.3 by
lamalab-org (MIT), extended in ReShiki, and includes OPSIN-derived data and
a CPython integer-set adaptation (Python-2.0). The perception kernel adapts
RDKit (BSD-3-Clause), SiMolecule centres (BSD-2-Clause), and LLVM libc++ and
Microsoft STL (Apache-2.0 WITH LLVM-exception). The root attribution records
listed above remain applicable to ReShiki's retained code, data and fixtures.
Historical crates/opsin/ and crates/nazuchem* paths in retained attribution
records identify source locations before the split; current naming sources
and provenance live in the external nazuchem repository at the pin above.

Binary distributions include a Licenses directory. It contains this notice,
the project license texts, and THIRD-PARTY-NOTICES.txt with the source
attribution records, dependency versions and declarations, and complete
upstream license texts and notices. Identical texts are included once,
with an explicit reference for each dependency and original source file.

On macOS these files are inside ReShiki.app/Contents/Resources/Licenses.
